{"id":"q26","run":1,"role":"calibration","category":"linda","question":"Tom is a quiet, tidy, detail-oriented man who loves science fiction and computers. Is it more probable that Tom is a software engineer who lives in Seattle than that Tom lives in Seattle?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"The description fits the stereotype of a software engineer.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The added detail makes the conjunction feel more coherent and so may seem more likely than the bare Seattle claim.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The question asks about probability, not fit with a stereotype.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The conjunction can only be true if Tom lives in Seattle is also true.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The probability of the conjunction can never exceed the probability of Tom living in Seattle.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Even if the description makes being a software engineer very likely, it cannot make the conjunction more probable than one of its parts.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The conjunction rule P(A and B) ≤ P(B) settles the question.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The description may raise the probability that Tom is a software engineer and make the story feel more representative.","confidence":0.7,"lean":1,"theta":1.0996,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The intuition that the more detailed option is more likely is the conjunction fallacy.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":-0.4,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":-0.8,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":-0.9,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":-0.